     "Implement the client shell for the short operations, so info, status, flash, erase, and reset reach a node over SSH.\n\nRefresh the image-generated private key from `http://<host>/probetron_key` before every operation and keep one host-specific copy under `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/probetron/keys/`.\nLeave a cache file whose bytes did not change alone, and replace a changed one through a temporary neighbour with mode 0600 and one atomic rename, so SSH never reads half a key.\nStop the operation, even when an older copy exists, on an HTTP failure, an empty answer, an answer without private-key framing, a cache file that anyone else can read, and a failed replacement.\n\nBuild every SSH invocation around the cached identity, `bench@<host>`, batch mode, no TTY, both known-host files at `/dev/null`, and a log level that drops the resulting new-host and changed-host warnings without hiding remote stderr, so a replaced Pi at the same address needs no cache cleanup.\nRun only `sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/probetron-node` remotely and serialize the validated node argv as one remote command whose tokens are quoted for a POSIX shell, so no public value can add a shell token however it passed validation.\nStream a flash ELF to remote standard input rather than naming a client path, and give back exactly the stdout, stderr, and status of the node, including its busy code, while naming the host when SSH itself never arrived.\n\nMake client information join the local Probetron and Babashka versions and the cache path it refreshed to the node report, in text or in EDN under stable client and node keys.\n\nSeparate the pure remote command, SSH argv, cache path, and report construction from the imperative code that owns HTTP, the filesystem, and subprocesses, and cover the whole client with tests that drive a local HTTP fixture and a fake SSH executable.",
