     :commit     "e669ebf06ed13f9b4540153a68f5cb21979dd9d9",
     :summary
     "Open the byte and RTT session on the client so that connect reaches a node target without an interactive login shell.\n\nAdd probetron.client.tunnel, the imperative shell of the long client sessions, which refreshes the same cached node key as a short operation, forwards the fixed node loopback byte port to an explicit --local-port or to a freshly reserved ephemeral client loopback port, prints the tcp:// endpoint before it waits for a host program, and holds the session until the outer node command ends.\n\nCarry the optional RTT ELF to the node on SSH standard input and leave both node streams attached to the client, so probe-rs diagnostics and decoded RTT text arrive unchanged and the node status becomes the client status.\n\nPresent the same endpoint as a pseudo-terminal when --pty asks for one and the client has socat, in a private volatile link directory that only the client may enter, and fall back to an actionable warning and the usable TCP endpoint when socat is missing.\n\nTreat a pseudo-terminal that closes as a lost presentation alone, and clean up once on completion or on a handled signal by stopping the local helper, removing the link directory, and ending the outer SSH process, which hangs the remote command up and gives the node target lock back; --reset-on-exit travels to the node inside the remote command and never becomes a client action.\n\nMove the node loopback service ports into the shared operation model, teach the shared SSH argv to carry the forward options, and let one function name the ELF that any operation uploads, so the client sends an RTT image the same way it sends firmware.\n\nCover the new behaviour with public client tests that drive stand-in SSH and socat programs, including a fixture client that holds a whole session in its own process for the handled-signal path.",
