     "Implement the locked connect session of the node on the lifecycle shell, so a client bridges the DUT byte channel and reads decoded RTT logs instead of receiving a missing-backend diagnostic.\n\nAdd probetron.node.session, which publishes the DUT byte channel through one socat listener bound to Pi loopback alone.\nThe listener reuses the address, allows one byte client at a time, and accepts the next client as soon as that one leaves, so byte clients reconnect in sequence for as long as the outer node operation lives.\nForking also reopens the channel address for every accepted connection, so a DUT that re-enumerated over USB resolves its stable path again.\nThe UART channel opens as a raw byte stream at the requested bit rate, while the USB channel waits up to the requested bounded interval for the DUT to enumerate.\nA channel device that is missing or unreadable names itself, its expected path, and the wiring or image rule that repairs it, and it stops the session before any helper starts.\n\nReceive and validate the optional RTT ELF through the same bounded volatile upload path that a flash uses, before the session opens any hardware, and then decode RTT with probe-rs attach over the explicit Linux SPI selector and the SWD protocol.\nAttach never downloads and never resets, so the decoder joins a running target.\nThe decoder and the listener are two independent owned process groups: DUT bytes stay on the TCP service, decoded text stays on node standard output and standard error, and a decoder that stops leaves the bridge usable.\nCleanup removes both groups and the RTT upload when the outer process exits or receives a handled signal, and it preserves DUT state unless the session asked for a reset on exit.\n\nExtend the pure hardware description with the byte-service, channel-address, and RTT command lines, with the UART and USB channel resources and their repairs, and with the diagnostic for a resource that exists but cannot be read.\nGive the runtime a rea