     :commit     "60bb338796fd8c5af17e173f86e3c029bf2c222d",
     :summary
     "Give the node an ownership shell that holds one nonblocking flock transaction around every hardware operation and owns the process groups that operation starts.\n\nAdd probetron.node.lifecycle as the pure ownership model: it says how long each operation owns the target, builds the volatile active-command record, validates a record before anyone trusts it, renders the status of the lock as text or EDN, and returns the argv of every appliance executable.\n\nAdd probetron.node.runner as the imperative shell around that model.\nIt takes /run/probetron/target.lock through /usr/bin/flock without waiting, holds it with a /bin/cat that lives exactly as long as the operation so that no lost session leaves the lock behind, and answers a conflict at once with the reserved busy status and the record of the owner.\nAfter it takes the lock it writes /run/probetron/active.edn through a temporary neighbour, so a reader sees the whole record or none of it, and it drops that record again in cleanup.\nIt starts every long-running helper through /usr/bin/setsid in its own process group, registers cleanup before the operation can start one, and sends SIGTERM and then SIGKILL through /bin/kill to those groups alone on normal completion, on a lost SSH session, and on SIGINT, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP.\nCleanup runs the reset that --reset-on-exit asks for only after every owned child is gone and before the lock goes back, and the default path leaves the target as it found it.\n\nCarry out status in the runner, which distinguishes a free lock from a held one with a shared probe that never opens the hardware, reports the active record, and drops a record that a free lock left behind.\nName a missing runtime directory or an unusable lock instead of guessing, and report it with a new unavailable exit status.\n\nGather the appliance paths, the fixed absolute executables, and the filesystem, clock, and process functions in one runtime map, so a test owns a temporary target while production always takes the fixed paths.\nWire the node entry point to that runtime and move the node program name into the shared lifecycle model.\n\nCover the shell with integration tests that drive the public runner against temporary paths and a fixture node process, and prove that a second process gets the busy status without waiting, that the record names its owner, that a handled termination reaps the fixture helper and the grandchild it left in the same group, that stale metadata never reports a false owner, and that reset-on-exit sees children that are already gone.",
