     :summary
     "Add persistent remote DAP sessions to the node and the client on the existing lifecycle and tunnel abstractions.\n\nMake the node debug session discover the udev-named SWD bus under /dev/spidev_swd*, announce the probe selector that a DAP client request repeats, and serve the pinned probe-rs DAP server on the fixed 127.0.0.1:50000 port with multi-session mode enabled, which probe-rs takes whenever --single-session is absent.\nKeep chip, speed, ELF, SVD, source, launch, and attach configuration out of the server argv and inside the DAP client request, so the probe-rs editor integration uploads client-local files and resolves client-local source against a node that knows no project.\nGeneralize the session wait over the byte service and the DAP service, so the outer debug command holds the target lock across every individual DAP disconnect and reconnect and releases it only when the outer command ends.\nRefuse a node without its SWD alias before any server starts, and name the device and the udev repair.\n\nMake the client forward the node DAP port to a selected or validated client loopback port, print that endpoint, and keep the outer SSH process alive until explicit termination.\nPreserve target state on DAP disconnect and on default cleanup, and let cleanup reap the whole DAP process group before the best-effort reset that --reset-on-exit asks for.\nDrop both unsupported-operation branches, because every public command now reaches hardware.\n\nCover the exact multi-session and loopback DAP argv, the discovery announcement and its missing-resource refusal, SSH forwarding and endpoint output, lock retention while a fake DAP client connects and leaves twice, handled termination, child cleanup, and reset ordering.\nTeach the node session fixture to stand in for a real multi-session listener on an ephemeral port, and give both session fixtures a command argument so one process can hold either long session.\n\nKeep the /dev/spidev0.0 selector that info, flash, erase, and RTT already use: the /dev/spidev_swd* alias names the same SPI0 bus, and reworking every short operation onto glob discovery belongs outside this subtask.",
